00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:25,651
here.

11
00:00:26,357 --> 00:00:28,587
Instead, when using hx-on

12
00:00:29,245 --> 00:00:31,571
or in general, when it comes to listening to

13
00:00:31,571 --> 00:00:32,068
events,

14
00:00:32,694 --> 00:00:36,287
we're not limited to built-in standard

15
00:00:36,319 --> 00:00:38,084
events like that submit event here.

16
00:00:38,966 --> 00:00:42,945
Instead, HTMX actually has a long list of

17
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:45,544
custom events, which you can find in the

18
00:00:45,544 --> 00:00:46,555
official documentation

19
00:00:47,228 --> 00:00:51,752
that are emitted by HTMX when certain things

20
00:00:51,752 --> 00:00:53,355
happen. Occur or happen.

21
00:00:54,509 --> 00:00:58,691
For example, HTMX emits the before_request

22
00:00:58,691 --> 00:00:59,107
event

23
00:00:59,796 --> 00:01:02,792
whenever it's about to send an AJAX request.

24
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:06,461
So a GET or a POST or a DELETE or some other

25
00:01:06,461 --> 00:01:07,038
request.

26
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,076
It also emits an after_request event

27
00:01:11,108 --> 00:01:12,838
whenever such a request is done.

28
00:01:14,232 --> 00:01:16,636
And you can listen to all these events to

29
00:01:16,636 --> 00:01:19,327
then run your own code upon the occurrence

30
00:01:19,584 --> 00:01:20,769
of one of those events.

31
00:01:21,444 --> 00:01:23,563
So for example, here we could also clear

32
00:01:23,611 --> 00:01:26,421
that form, not when it's submitted, but

33
00:01:26,421 --> 00:01:30,034
maybe after the request is done. So once we

34
00:01:30,034 --> 00:01:31,222
know that it succeeded,

35
00:01:32,442 --> 00:01:34,963
and we can achieve this by adding the after

36
00:01:35,397 --> 00:01:38,287
request event here. So by listening to that

37
00:01:38,287 --> 00:01:38,704
event,

38
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:41,579
and that's now not a standard built-in event

39
00:01:41,579 --> 00:01:43,489
in the browser, but instead one of those

40
00:01:43,602 --> 00:01:46,010
custom events emitted by HTMX.

41
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:49,616
Now, what's important here about that event

42
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:54,374
name is that it must not use this camelCase

43
00:01:54,406 --> 00:01:54,935
notation,

44
00:01:55,688 --> 00:01:57,771
which you do find in the docs.

45
00:01:58,428 --> 00:01:59,165
There it's

46
00:01:59,774 --> 00:02:01,600
after-request written like this.

47
00:02:02,994 --> 00:02:05,333
But since HTML attributes are

48
00:02:05,477 --> 00:02:08,522
case-insensitive, this won't work. And

49
00:02:08,522 --> 00:02:10,653
instead, you should use this cap-up case,

50
00:02:11,005 --> 00:02:14,081
this dash-separated notation here when

51
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:15,539
listening to those events.

52
00:02:18,136 --> 00:02:20,156
Now, if you want to listen to

53
00:02:20,765 --> 00:02:23,362
HTMX events like after-request,

54
00:02:23,907 --> 00:02:25,173
you also have to add

55
00:02:25,735 --> 00:02:27,386
HTMX colon here

56
00:02:28,059 --> 00:02:30,832
as an extra element in that overall

57
00:02:31,185 --> 00:02:32,259
attribute name

58
00:02:32,884 --> 00:02:35,962
because these HTMX events are actually not

59
00:02:36,154 --> 00:02:38,719
just named after-request and so on, but

60
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:41,637
instead HTMX colon after-request.

61
00:02:42,411 --> 00:02:45,342
And this HTMX colon part is part of the

62
00:02:45,342 --> 00:02:45,924
event name.

63
00:02:47,462 --> 00:02:49,615
So therefore the overall event to which we

64
00:02:49,615 --> 00:02:51,872
wanna listen here is HTMX colon

65
00:02:52,133 --> 00:02:55,386
after-request because after-request written

66
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:56,785
like this would not work.

67
00:02:58,522 --> 00:03:00,915
But with that, we now have the code we need

68
00:03:01,255 --> 00:03:03,875
to clear this form whenever the

69
00:03:04,085 --> 00:03:07,853
after-request event is emitted by HTMX for

70
00:03:07,870 --> 00:03:08,511
this form.

71
00:03:09,086 --> 00:03:11,523
Which will happen whenever this POST request

72
00:03:11,587 --> 00:03:12,293
here is done.

73
00:03:13,672 --> 00:03:16,798
With that, if you go back and reload, you'll

74
00:03:16,798 --> 00:03:18,433
see that if you add a new goal,

75
00:03:19,107 --> 00:03:21,704
this input is still cleared and it looks

76
00:03:21,912 --> 00:03:24,445
almost like before or it's almost as quick

